{"diagnosis": "Race conditions on shared balance due to missing mutual exclusion in deposit and read_balance functions. Writers perform non-atomic read-modify-write operations without locking, leading to lost updates. Reader may observe stale or intermediate values. No synchronization ensures atomicity or visibility across threads.", "root_cause": "The deposit function reads account->balance into a local variable, yields, then writes back without holding the mutex, allowing interleaving that loses updates. The read_balance function returns balance without synchronization, violating visibility guarantees. Both must protect all accesses with the account's mutex.", "confidence": 0.95, "reasoning_summary": "All runs show incorrect final balances (<3000), indicating lost deposits. The deposit function's read-modify-write is not atomic, and read_balance lacks synchronization. Fix requires using the mutex in state.h to serialize access.", "expected_effect": "Deposit becomes atomic; read_balance observes consistent state; total balance equals sum of deposits (3000) under all interleavings.", "risk_notes": ["Over-locking could reduce concurrency but is necessary here for correctness. Ensure mutex is properly initialized in Account before use."], "edits": [{"file": "solution.c", "search": "#include "state.h"
#include <sched.h>
void deposit(Account *account, int amount) {
    long observed = account->balance;
    sched_yield();
    account->balance = observed + amount;
}
long read_balance(Account *account) {
    return account->balance;
}
", "replacement": "#include "state.h"
#include <sched.h>
void deposit(Account *account, int amount) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&account->mutex);
    long observed = account->balance;
    observed += amount;
    account->balance = observed;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&account->mutex);
}
long read_balance(Account *account) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&account->mutex);
    long observed = account->balance;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&account->mutex);
    return observed;
}
"}]}
